From: Alex Scotti <alexscotti@gmail.com>
Subject: vh265: say what state the decoder timed out in

timeout_process() logs only HEVC_MPC_E, which does not say which of the two
timeout paths fired, and they mean opposite things.

The watchdog runs at decode_timeout_val (200ms). When dec_status is
HEVC_CODED_SLICE_SEGMENT_DAT it compares the LCU decode position against the
previous sample: real progress calls restart_process_time() and it takes two
strikes to fire, so firing there means the decoder genuinely stopped mid-slice.
Every OTHER state fires IMMEDIATELY at 200ms with no progress check and no
strike count - including, as far as the register values go, a decoder that is
simply waiting for input that has not arrived yet.

That distinction decides whether a timeout is a stuck decoder or a starved one,
and on this rig the timeouts were seen only under heavy trick play on SMB
sources, where input starvation after a seek is expected. Guessing between the
two from HEVC_MPC_E alone is not possible, so log the terms: the decoder status
word, the LCU index then and now, how long the window actually ran, and the
slice count.

Diagnostic only - no behaviour change.

---
--- a/drivers/frame_provider/decoder/h265/vh265.c
+++ b/drivers/frame_provider/decoder/h265/vh265.c
@@ -13760,8 +13760,16 @@
 
 static void timeout_process(struct hevc_state_s *hevc)
 {
-	hevc_print(hevc, 0, "%s decoder timeout HEVC_MPC_E=0x%x\n",
-		__func__, READ_VREG(HEVC_MPC_E));
+	hevc_print(hevc, 0,
+		"%s decoder timeout HEVC_MPC_E=0x%x dec_status=0x%x "
+		"lcu_was=%d lcu_now=%d elapsed=%ums slices=%d\n",
+		__func__, READ_VREG(HEVC_MPC_E),
+		READ_VREG(HEVC_DEC_STATUS_REG),
+		hevc->last_lcu_idx,
+		READ_VREG(HEVC_PARSER_LCU_START) & 0xffffff,
+		hevc->start_process_time ?
+			(u32)(1000 * (jiffies - hevc->start_process_time) / HZ) : 0,
+		hevc->slice_count);
 
 	reset_process_time(hevc);
 
